questions.answer:
async function getJSON(url) {
  const res = await fetch(url, { headers: { Accept: "application/json" } });
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status} for ${url}`);
  if (res.status === 204) return null;
  return res.json();
}

getJSON("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/1")
  .then((todo) => console.log(todo.title))
  .catch((err) => console.error(err.message));
// delectus aut autem

The ok check converts an HTTP error into a rejected promise so one catch handles it. The 204 branch matters because an empty body makes res.json() throw SyntaxError, not return null. Network failures reject before the status check and reach the same catch. If the server can return 200 with non-JSON (an HTML login page, for example), read res.text() and parse inside try/catch, or check the content-type header first. Including the URL in the message makes logs actionable.

